WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.910
Sometimes you can tell if two animals are
related just by looking at them.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:08.250
It’s no surprise dogs and wolves share a
common ancestor.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:11.650
Or that tree squirrels and chipmunks are in
the same family.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:17.079
But occasionally, two different species share
a relation which stretches the mind.

00:00:17.079 --> 00:00:21.660
So, for this entry into the archive, let’s
take a look at the animals you’d never guess

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.630
would show up to the same family reunion.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.820
To start off, we have rhinos and horses.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:32.830
Just looking at the multi-ton, leathery rhino,
you might sooner guess it’s a cousin of

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:39.740
the elephant or hippopotamus.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.250
How is this possible?

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:46.910
Well, while the exact common ancestor of these
two has yet to be found, we know both rhinos

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:54.190
and horses are distantly related to the same
small forest dwelling predecessor called Hyracotherium.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.500
The genus lived during the Eocene period.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.850
I’ve got more on them and other tiny early
mammals in another video.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:05.970
Today, the hulking rhino and majestic horses
have clearly gone in different adaptational

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.280
directions.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:13.040
Despite this, both species still belong to
the same larger category: the odd toed ungulates:

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.000
a classification which also includes the tapir.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.390
That’s right — there’s a third branch
in this family tree.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:23.970
The tapir is a squat, somewhat odd-looking
herbivore with a short trunk like and elephant

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:29.700
for grasping at food — despite also having
little relation to actual elephants.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.880
Looking at these three side-by-side, they
hardly look similar, but that’s the funny

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:35.920
thing about adaptation.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.340
Up next, we have seals and bears.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:43.020
The cuddly aquatic seal might appear far off
from the large, terrestrial bear.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.580
But genetically speaking, the two mammal groups
are pretty closely related.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:51.619
It’s believed both animals share a common
ancestor in the Pujila — an extinct species

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:53.350
of semi-aquatic mammal.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:58.220
Over the course of millions of years, some
members of this ancestral species spent more

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:02.620
and more time in the water: and became the
almost fully aquatic group we recognize as

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:04.110
seals today.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:08.580
Other remained mostly on land and became larger,
heavier, and more diverse until they developed

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.040
into the various modern bear species we’re
now familiar with.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:15.150
But biologically speaking, the two are still
cousins.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:18.859
Continuing on, we have the hippopotamus and
the dolphin.

00:02:18.859 --> 00:02:24.700
The four limbed, heavy-set hippo and the sleek,
flippered dolphin might seem like total opposites

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.069
at first glance.

00:02:26.069 --> 00:02:30.129
But like many of the other pairs on this list,
they share a common ancestor.

00:02:30.129 --> 00:02:33.260
The two groups split off around 60 million
years ago.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:37.760
And the group that eventually gave us the
dolphin: the cetaceans, also includes all

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.209
species of porpoises and whales.

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:44.590
Meaning the whale and hippopotamus are, incredibly,
also related.

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:45.879
What a curious lineage.

00:02:45.879 --> 00:02:50.659
Next, we’ve got the elephant: which, as
we’ve already established, isn’t as closely

00:02:50.659 --> 00:02:55.109
related to other African Megafauna like rhinos
or hippos as you might expect.

00:02:55.109 --> 00:03:00.420
So, what are the closest living relatives
of the largest land animal on planet earth?

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:04.430
The rock hyrax— a species of tiny, rodent
like mammals.

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:06.029
And the manatee.

00:03:06.029 --> 00:03:10.709
At the very least, manatees are a hefty, leathery
group of animals — so you can somewhat see

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:13.859
how such a species might be related to the
elephant.

00:03:13.859 --> 00:03:18.739
But the tiny, furry rock hyrax seems like
quite the odd relative to the heaviest thing

00:03:18.739 --> 00:03:20.439
on the African savanna.

00:03:20.439 --> 00:03:24.370
Part of how we know these two species are
related is the makeup of the Hyrax’s front

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:27.819
teeth is actually similar to that of an elephant’s
tusks.

00:03:27.819 --> 00:03:33.819
Indeed, the two species only diverged around
65 million years ago, which isn’t that impossibly

00:03:33.819 --> 00:03:36.719
long on an evolutionary timescale.

00:03:36.719 --> 00:03:37.719
Pretty interesting.

00:03:37.719 --> 00:03:40.849
Moving on, we have the echidna and the platypus.

00:03:40.849 --> 00:03:44.680
While these two don’t look ‘alike,’
they are perhaps the most made up-seeming

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:46.450
organisms alive today.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:49.400
So, it kind of tracks they’d be related.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:53.329
Both groups are the only mammals currently
living which lay eggs.

00:03:53.329 --> 00:03:58.639
Even stranger, however, the platypus is able
to detect electric fields with its bill — and

00:03:58.639 --> 00:04:00.829
have venomous elbows.

00:04:00.829 --> 00:04:05.359
Like the platypus, the echidna can also detect
electric fields in its bill through its snout,

00:04:05.359 --> 00:04:08.479
which is part of links the two mammals to
each other.

00:04:08.479 --> 00:04:10.479
What an incredible pair.

00:04:10.479 --> 00:04:14.870
Nearing the end, we have the giraffe and the
pronghorn antelope.

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:19.640
Giraffes are such unique mammals in general,
with their extremely long necks and characteristic

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:24.630
spots, that you might not assume anything
— let alone an antelope would be their relatives.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:29.150
And indeed, despite their name, pronghorn
antelopes aren’t actually antelopes at all

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:33.040
— but an offshoot species more closely linked
to the giraffe.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:38.450
This relation is a relatively recent scientific
discover — and frankly, it’s almost nice

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:42.470
to know the truly unusual giraffes have a
genetic relative.

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:46.820
They might still stick out as the tallest
living terrestrial animal, but as it turns

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:50.020
out, they’re not alone, biologically speaking.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:52.800
Finally, we have jellyfish and coral.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:58.320
Since jellyfish are free-floating, ethereal
animals, and coral are so sedentary they hardly

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:02.650
seem like animals at all, you’d be forgiven
for assuming they have nothing in common.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:08.330
But in fact, both jellyfish and coral are
cnidarians: aquatic animals with specialized

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:10.240
cells for capturing prey.

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:14.850
You might not think of coral as hunting prey
at all, but in fact, some species do possess

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:20.190
stinging tentacles like jellyfish, which they
use to ensnare floating zooplankton that gets

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.220
too close.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:26.480
Indeed, some coral have even been observed
consuming jellyfish — which seems quite

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:27.480
surprising.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:34.830
And that’s the end of our analysis of animals
you might not have known were related.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:38.910
